(Bug #33 - `is empty` on a text was always false: the predicate tested
 the pointer, not the contents. A text's pointer is never null, so even
 `"" is empty` answered false. Lists and buffers were always correct -
 their rows here are the controls that pin the defect to text. The
 undefaulted flag row guards LANGUAGE.md's promise that an unsupplied
 text flag holds "" and can be tested with `is empty`.)
a flag called label is "-l" or "--label", it is a text.
a flag called title is "-t" or "--title", it is a text with default "hi".
Parse flags.

a text called blank is "".
a text called filled is "x".
a list called nolist is [].
a list called somelist is [1].
Create a buffer called nobuf.
a buffer called somebuf is "y".

If blank is empty then, Print "blank:empty". Otherwise, Print "blank:not".
If filled is empty then, Print "filled:empty". Otherwise, Print "filled:not".
If label is empty then, Print "flag-no-default:empty". Otherwise, Print "flag-no-default:not".
If title is empty then, Print "flag-default:empty". Otherwise, Print "flag-default:not".
If nolist is empty then, Print "empty-list:empty". Otherwise, Print "empty-list:not".
If somelist is empty then, Print "full-list:empty". Otherwise, Print "full-list:not".
If nobuf is empty then, Print "empty-buffer:empty". Otherwise, Print "empty-buffer:not".
If somebuf is empty then, Print "full-buffer:empty". Otherwise, Print "full-buffer:not".

While blank is empty,
  Print "while:sees-empty",
  Break.
